WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.810
A strong counter-deception review begins by separating three things that often get blended

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:05.130
together:

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.120
the claim, the source, and the evidence.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:10.530
The claim is what someone says is true or wants the organization to believe.

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:14.480
The source is where the claim came from, including a person, system, vendor, report,

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:15.835
account, or channel.

00:00:15.835 --> 00:00:18.020
The evidence is the artifact, telemetry, record,

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:20.795
or observation offered to support the claim.

00:00:20.795 --> 00:00:24.035
Reliability and credibility are related, but they are not the same.

00:00:24.035 --> 00:00:27.955
Reliability describes the source over time: does this source usually provide accurate,

00:00:27.955 --> 00:00:30.475
complete, and responsibly handled information?

00:00:30.475 --> 00:00:33.040
Credibility describes the specific information in front of you:

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.740
is this claim internally consistent, supported by evidence,

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:38.255
and compatible with independently observed context?

00:00:38.255 --> 00:00:40.125
A usually reliable source can be wrong,

00:00:40.125 --> 00:00:43.680
and an unfamiliar source can provide information worth checking.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.480
Provenance is the record of where evidence came from and how it was handled.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:50.820
For a log export, provenance may include the system, query, time range, account used,

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:51.784
and collection method.

00:00:51.784 --> 00:00:55.085
For a screenshot, it may include who captured it, when it was captured,

00:00:55.085 --> 00:00:57.785
what view it came from, and whether the underlying system can

00:00:57.785 --> 00:00:58.640
still be checked.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.060
Provenance helps analysts distinguish an original artifact from a copy, summary,

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:04.800
transformation, or claim about an artifact.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.060
Corroboration improves confidence when independent sources point in the same direction.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:09.795
Independence matters.

00:01:09.795 --> 00:01:13.535
Three messages copied from the same original source do not provide the same value as a log

00:01:13.535 --> 00:01:13.845
record,

00:01:13.845 --> 00:01:15.500
a ticket history, and an approved

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:18.594
workflow record that each support the same conclusion.

00:01:18.594 --> 00:01:21.910
By separating claim, source, evidence, provenance, and corroboration,

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:24.675
analysts can communicate what is solid, what is plausible,

00:01:24.675 --> 00:01:26.480
and what still needs verification.
